WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:03.530 --> 00:00:05.100
Welcome to Ear Biscuits,

00:00:05.100 --> 00:00:07.450
the podcast where two
lifelong friends talk about

00:00:07.450 --> 00:00:10.320
life for a long time, I'm Rhett...

00:00:10.320 --> 00:00:11.400
And I'm Link.

00:00:11.400 --> 00:00:14.410
This week at the round
table of dim lighting,

00:00:14.410 --> 00:00:17.910
Rhett's taking me on a
cross-country road trip.

00:00:17.910 --> 00:00:18.850
Vroom, vroom.

00:00:18.850 --> 00:00:21.280
I've been waiting to hear...

00:00:21.280 --> 00:00:22.200
Ah, I bet you have.

00:00:22.200 --> 00:00:24.400
About this Road-trip.

00:00:24.400 --> 00:00:26.527
Well, the moment I heard,

00:00:26.527 --> 00:00:29.000
when I was back here in LA,

00:00:29.000 --> 00:00:30.472
you were still in North Carolina,

00:00:30.472 --> 00:00:35.472
your trip to Mexico was disintegrating

00:00:36.840 --> 00:00:39.060
right before your eyes

00:00:39.060 --> 00:00:41.400
and I was feeling sorry for you,

00:00:41.400 --> 00:00:44.170
and then all of a sudden, Christie's like,

00:00:44.170 --> 00:00:45.881
because she's reading
the thread and I'm not

00:00:45.881 --> 00:00:47.750
because I'm in full vacation mode.

00:00:47.750 --> 00:00:49.760
She's like "Rhett and Locke,

00:00:49.760 --> 00:00:51.650
they're gonna drive back to LA."

00:00:51.650 --> 00:00:53.653
they're doing a cross-country road trip.

00:00:53.653 --> 00:00:54.980
Best idea ever.

00:00:54.980 --> 00:00:56.470
I've got admit...

00:00:56.470 --> 00:00:57.670
Jealous.

00:00:57.670 --> 00:00:59.580
Yeah, it's like immediate jealousy.

00:00:59.580 --> 00:01:01.850
Like this sinking side, It's like...

00:01:03.030 --> 00:01:04.853
Well, why didn't they invite me?

00:01:06.125 --> 00:01:08.150
Just wait until you had all the story.

00:01:08.150 --> 00:01:09.270
If you invited me,

00:01:09.270 --> 00:01:11.910
that's not how cross-country trips work,

00:01:11.910 --> 00:01:13.080
I was on the other coast already,

00:01:13.080 --> 00:01:13.913
that's why you didn't invite me.

00:01:13.913 --> 00:01:14.810
I could've picked you up

00:01:14.810 --> 00:01:18.210
and taken you to my house in
the last like half mile or so.

00:01:18.210 --> 00:01:22.770
No, but like, the whole
week after Christmas

00:01:22.770 --> 00:01:24.160
that we were all on break,

00:01:24.160 --> 00:01:26.187
I was like, "Let's do something guys."

00:01:26.187 --> 00:01:28.170
And I could always rent a van again,

00:01:28.170 --> 00:01:30.916
and we can do a couple of nights camping.

00:01:30.916 --> 00:01:32.800
There was like torrential...

00:01:32.800 --> 00:01:33.633
It was raining like crazy here.

00:01:33.633 --> 00:01:36.890
Pouring of rain here, it was depressing,

00:01:36.890 --> 00:01:40.670
didn't want to go out,
but it was relaxing.

00:01:40.670 --> 00:01:43.570
But I wanted to do something

00:01:43.570 --> 00:01:46.470
and they wanted it to just chill out.

00:01:46.470 --> 00:01:49.050
I respected that, but I
was a little frustrated.

00:01:49.050 --> 00:01:50.670
And then on top of that, it's like,

00:01:50.670 --> 00:01:53.580
man, he's got this like father-son thing.

00:01:53.580 --> 00:01:54.420
Bonding.

00:01:54.420 --> 00:01:59.160
seeing places, I've only
done one cross-country trip.

00:01:59.160 --> 00:02:00.390
And it wasn't when my son...

00:02:00.390 --> 00:02:01.223
It was with me.

00:02:01.223 --> 00:02:02.260
Yeah, it was.

00:02:02.260 --> 00:02:03.733
Similar route that I took.

00:02:04.600 --> 00:02:06.370
So I want to hear all about it.

00:02:06.370 --> 00:02:07.970
You've been saving it

00:02:07.970 --> 00:02:09.320
to tell me on this podcast.

00:02:10.440 --> 00:02:15.440
Even a couple of times,
we've been amongst friends

00:02:16.180 --> 00:02:18.070
who wanted to hear about the
trip and you've been there

00:02:18.070 --> 00:02:20.980
and I had to say, I can't talk
about it because Link's here.

00:02:20.980 --> 00:02:23.470
People are like, "These
guys are so weird."

00:02:23.470 --> 00:02:25.240
I can't tell you about my trip

00:02:25.240 --> 00:02:27.160
because I have to wait until now.

00:02:27.160 --> 00:02:28.810
I mean, they understood.

00:02:28.810 --> 00:02:29.767
They laughed because I was like,

00:02:29.767 --> 00:02:34.140
"Listen, I don't want to
have to act not bored,

00:02:34.140 --> 00:02:36.710
hearing it a second time on my podcast."

00:02:36.710 --> 00:02:39.724
That's more important than
us talking about it now,

00:02:39.724 --> 00:02:40.623
than our friendship.

00:02:40.623 --> 00:02:42.360
Hopefully, you're the kind of

00:02:42.360 --> 00:02:44.500
Ear Biscuits here/Mythical Beast

00:02:44.500 --> 00:02:46.730
who listens to every
episode of Ear Biscuits

00:02:46.730 --> 00:02:49.700
so you already know that COVID changed

00:02:49.700 --> 00:02:51.550
my plans to go to Mexico,

00:02:51.550 --> 00:02:53.050
and then I decided to go on a road trip.

00:02:53.050 --> 00:02:55.870
I'm not going to tell any more than that.

00:02:55.870 --> 00:02:57.310
But I wanted it...

00:02:57.310 --> 00:02:58.570
Why was it me and Locke?

00:02:58.570 --> 00:03:01.000
Like why was me and my 17-year-old son

00:03:01.000 --> 00:03:02.570
and not my 13-year-old son?

00:03:02.570 --> 00:03:05.530
Well, my 13-year-old son,

00:03:05.530 --> 00:03:08.170
very likely got COVID over the break

00:03:08.170 --> 00:03:10.970
and that's why we just
didn't go to Mexico.

00:03:10.970 --> 00:03:14.610
And so Locke and I have been planning

00:03:14.610 --> 00:03:17.140
on taking a trip together, maybe during...

00:03:17.140 --> 00:03:18.150
We've been trying to get go...

00:03:18.150 --> 00:03:20.587
Can we close that loop? He's fine.

00:03:20.587 --> 00:03:22.500
Yeah, yeah. Shepherd's totally fine.

00:03:22.500 --> 00:03:25.550
Shepherd is double vaccinated

00:03:25.550 --> 00:03:27.963
so he experienced essentially a cold,

00:03:29.710 --> 00:03:33.380
he was down for a couple
of days and he's back.

00:03:33.380 --> 00:03:35.170
We never confirmed that that's what he had

00:03:35.170 --> 00:03:37.160
because all we had was those at-home tests

00:03:37.160 --> 00:03:39.720
and so we're just assuming
that's what he had.

00:03:39.720 --> 00:03:40.750
But if it's me,

00:03:40.750 --> 00:03:41.583
and I'm not going to put

00:03:41.583 --> 00:03:42.900
these thoughts and words in your mouth

00:03:42.900 --> 00:03:45.410
and I don't want to throw you
under your family bus here,

00:03:45.410 --> 00:03:48.544
but if it were me and I was in
your situation at that point,

00:03:48.544 --> 00:03:49.377
I would've been like,

00:03:49.377 --> 00:03:51.410
"This presents a unique opportunity

00:03:51.410 --> 00:03:54.590
for me and Lincoln to
travel across the country,

00:03:54.590 --> 00:03:56.250
without the other siblings."

00:03:56.250 --> 00:03:59.670
I mean, doing a cross-country
road trip as a family

00:03:59.670 --> 00:04:01.080
is a totally different thing

00:04:01.080 --> 00:04:03.010
than just doing it with one son,

00:04:03.010 --> 00:04:04.010
it's a different concept.

00:04:04.010 --> 00:04:05.259
We actually considered.

00:04:05.259 --> 00:04:08.380
It was on the table for us to all go

00:04:08.380 --> 00:04:09.800
because actually by the time we left

00:04:09.800 --> 00:04:11.550
Shepherd was essentially recovered,

00:04:12.390 --> 00:04:14.660
but Locke and I specifically,

00:04:14.660 --> 00:04:16.038
have been trying to plan a trip together.

00:04:16.038 --> 00:04:18.480
We were going to go to Japan

00:04:18.480 --> 00:04:20.500
and then we were going to go to,

00:04:20.500 --> 00:04:21.790
I don't know, somewhere else.

00:04:21.790 --> 00:04:24.750
And then, COVID has changed
our plans multiple times

00:04:24.750 --> 00:04:27.160
and so, this is a trip
that's been in the making,

00:04:27.160 --> 00:04:29.372
and he's going to graduate
high school this year...

00:04:29.372 --> 00:04:31.600
So it's not about avoiding

00:04:31.600 --> 00:04:34.440
spending time with your
whole family in one car,

00:04:34.440 --> 00:04:36.440
but that I had to think that was

00:04:36.440 --> 00:04:38.130
a little bit of a fringe benefit.

00:04:38.130 --> 00:04:41.500
Well, all it took, as
Jessie and I discussed it

00:04:41.500 --> 00:04:44.367
for about 15 seconds, as I was like,

00:04:44.367 --> 00:04:48.760
"You know how our family is so intense,

00:04:48.760 --> 00:04:51.290
that there's a chance that
all four of us in a car

00:04:51.290 --> 00:04:52.870
could disintegrate really quickly?"

00:04:52.870 --> 00:04:53.703
I mean, there was a chance that

00:04:53.703 --> 00:04:56.210
me and Locke in car could
disintegrate very quickly,

00:04:56.210 --> 00:04:58.120
but the chances were lower.

00:04:58.120 --> 00:05:00.867
Now, interestingly, I've
talked a little bit about Locke

00:05:00.867 --> 00:05:02.460
and his tendency to be...

00:05:02.460 --> 00:05:04.810
He's an Enneagram Eight,
he's a challenger,

00:05:04.810 --> 00:05:06.910
and so one of the things that I have found

00:05:06.910 --> 00:05:10.420
to be consistently true
is when you have an idea,

00:05:10.420 --> 00:05:13.400
even if it's a great idea,
like a cross-country road trip,

00:05:13.400 --> 00:05:15.340
his initial reaction to it

00:05:15.340 --> 00:05:19.873
will be one of doubt and skepticism.

00:05:19.873 --> 00:05:21.110
It's always instinctive.

00:05:21.110 --> 00:05:23.780
Oh yeah, and I knew that
was going to happen.

00:05:23.780 --> 00:05:27.317
And lo and behold, when
I pitched the idea of,

00:05:27.317 --> 00:05:29.330
"Hey, man, day after Christmas,

00:05:29.330 --> 00:05:31.867
let's just drive back to Los Angeles."

00:05:32.770 --> 00:05:33.917
It wasn't an immediate, like,

00:05:33.917 --> 00:05:34.750
"This is a great idea."

00:05:34.750 --> 00:05:38.330
It was like, "How many hours

00:05:38.330 --> 00:05:39.510
is that going to be in the car?"

00:05:39.510 --> 00:05:41.363
Like these were the questions, right?

00:05:41.363 --> 00:05:42.789
It's a legit question.

00:05:42.789 --> 00:05:45.020
I don't want to spend too
much time on this point

00:05:45.020 --> 00:05:46.620
because obviously we ended up going

00:05:46.620 --> 00:05:49.600
but the way that I kind
of went into a dad-pitch

00:05:49.600 --> 00:05:52.840
and Jessie was involved and we
did a little bit of good cop,

00:05:52.840 --> 00:05:54.283
actually good cop, good cop.

00:05:55.120 --> 00:05:56.540
But I just said,

00:05:56.540 --> 00:06:00.580
listen, it's very difficult
for the future Locke

00:06:00.580 --> 00:06:04.710
to understand what is
being proposed right now.

00:06:04.710 --> 00:06:09.310
And that is, cross-country
trips of any kind...

00:06:09.310 --> 00:06:11.540
Well, future Locke is not
here, he's in the future.

00:06:11.540 --> 00:06:14.310
Right, you know what I mean?

00:06:14.310 --> 00:06:18.690
It is very difficult for
your future-self to look...

00:06:18.690 --> 00:06:20.430
You can't look back and appreciate

00:06:20.430 --> 00:06:22.780
what you are about to experience
in a cross-country trip.

00:06:22.780 --> 00:06:25.540
And also as someone who
has lived in adult life

00:06:25.540 --> 00:06:28.400
for some time, I know how hard it is

00:06:28.400 --> 00:06:29.990
to come by a cross-country trip.

00:06:29.990 --> 00:06:32.230
These aren't things that
just present themselves,

00:06:32.230 --> 00:06:34.847
but I've never met someone who was like,

00:06:34.847 --> 00:06:36.750
"I regret my cross-country trip.

00:06:36.750 --> 00:06:37.583
I really regret it."

00:06:37.583 --> 00:06:39.630
Now, I'm sure there are people who do,

00:06:39.630 --> 00:06:40.940
but most people are like,

00:06:40.940 --> 00:06:43.140
when I think about some of
the formative experiences,

00:06:43.140 --> 00:06:45.610
when I think about some of
the things that I remember

00:06:45.610 --> 00:06:47.980
in my life, oh, that cross-country trip.

00:06:47.980 --> 00:06:51.110
Like these are formative
experiences and memories

00:06:51.110 --> 00:06:51.943
and I was like,

00:06:51.943 --> 00:06:53.300
"You're not going to regret going on this.

00:06:53.300 --> 00:06:55.127
You will regret saying no, potentially."

00:06:55.127 --> 00:06:57.080
So you sold the experience

00:06:57.080 --> 00:06:59.310
in your future-self will thank you.

00:06:59.310 --> 00:07:00.760
Yeah, I've had to do that
a number of times with him.

00:07:00.760 --> 00:07:01.627
I mean, did you say,

00:07:01.627 --> 00:07:02.930
"And you know what,

00:07:02.930 --> 00:07:05.220
we're going to be announcing our location

00:07:05.220 --> 00:07:08.940
and meeting with fans
of mine at rest stops

00:07:08.940 --> 00:07:12.193
off the interstate all across America....

00:07:13.170 --> 00:07:14.150
All I did was...

00:07:14.150 --> 00:07:15.670
It will severely slow down our progress.

00:07:15.670 --> 00:07:18.847
As we stopped at multiple rest stops.

00:07:18.847 --> 00:07:21.573
During the first rest-stop stop I did say,

00:07:22.950 --> 00:07:24.950
there was a point in
time about a decade ago

00:07:24.950 --> 00:07:26.850
where Link and I did a cross country trip

00:07:26.850 --> 00:07:29.906
and stopped and announced to our fans

00:07:29.906 --> 00:07:31.870
that we were stopping
at various rest stops.

00:07:31.870 --> 00:07:34.617
And instead of thinking
it was weird he was like,

00:07:34.617 --> 00:07:35.800
"That's cool, man."

00:07:35.800 --> 00:07:36.670
It was cool.

00:07:36.670 --> 00:07:37.503
It was.

00:07:37.503 --> 00:07:38.420
If you're interested in that,

00:07:38.420 --> 00:07:40.550
you can just Google Mythical Road Trip.

00:07:40.550 --> 00:07:45.380
The videos are spread across
two different channels.

00:07:45.380 --> 00:07:46.650
Of course this is pre-GMM,

00:07:46.650 --> 00:07:50.083
so, across the GMM channel
and the main channel.

00:07:52.120 --> 00:07:53.677
Do some work to piece it
together for yourself,

00:07:53.677 --> 00:07:54.991
maybe there's a playlist.

00:07:54.991 --> 00:07:57.490
I'm going to be,

00:07:57.490 --> 00:07:59.640
I took a lot of video,
took a lot of pictures,

00:07:59.640 --> 00:08:03.190
including we took a couple of
those disposable film cameras

00:08:03.190 --> 00:08:04.560
that apparently take a week to develop,

00:08:04.560 --> 00:08:06.190
that's why they're not developed here.

00:08:06.190 --> 00:08:07.170
Oh, like a wedding.

00:08:07.170 --> 00:08:10.730
Yeah, but between all
of that visual media,

00:08:10.730 --> 00:08:14.240
if you're just listening to the podcast,

00:08:14.240 --> 00:08:16.450
just know that the YouTube version of this

00:08:16.450 --> 00:08:18.340
is going to have a lot
of that visual stuff.

00:08:18.340 --> 00:08:21.890
I'll show you some, if it's
needed for context, as we go.

00:08:21.890 --> 00:08:22.723
I'll watch the video,

00:08:22.723 --> 00:08:23.556
Or you can just watch later.

00:08:23.556 --> 00:08:24.626
I'll watch the YouTube video.

00:08:24.626 --> 00:08:27.300
But I don't want to spend
a lot of time on my phone.

00:08:27.300 --> 00:08:29.480
How much planning did you have?

00:08:29.480 --> 00:08:31.786
So, you sold him?

00:08:31.786 --> 00:08:32.922
Pick up where you left off here.

00:08:32.922 --> 00:08:34.580
He was like "I'll do it, dad."

00:08:34.580 --> 00:08:36.060
We were going to leave on the 26th.

00:08:36.060 --> 00:08:38.260
Did he have any
stipulations, was he like...

00:08:38.260 --> 00:08:39.730
Well, one of the things that he had to do

00:08:39.730 --> 00:08:42.110
is he had, he had two things to do,

00:08:42.110 --> 00:08:47.110
he's taking a German course
that's outside of his school

00:08:47.655 --> 00:08:51.140
because before he transferred
high schools a long time ago

00:08:51.140 --> 00:08:52.900
he was he was in the German curriculum

00:08:52.900 --> 00:08:55.070
and so he needs to finish
to like, get to like,

00:08:55.070 --> 00:08:56.382
think he's doing it, AP German,

00:08:56.382 --> 00:08:57.215
I don't know what it is.

00:08:57.215 --> 00:08:58.827
Was it like car
manufacturing, what was it?

00:08:58.827 --> 00:09:00.410
It was just the language of Germany.

00:09:00.410 --> 00:09:01.432
Oh, just the language.

00:09:01.432 --> 00:09:03.910
And so he needed to do that

00:09:03.910 --> 00:09:07.180
because he's got to finish
it by like mid-January

00:09:07.180 --> 00:09:09.547
so he needed to spend
some time doing homework.

00:09:09.547 --> 00:09:12.700
He's also currently
applying to more colleges

00:09:12.700 --> 00:09:14.030
than the ones he's already applied to

00:09:14.030 --> 00:09:17.080
so we needed to do some
essays and stuff like that.

00:09:17.080 --> 00:09:19.330
And I was like, "Listen,
we've got this giant SUV

00:09:19.330 --> 00:09:22.135
that's way too big for us,
it's super comfortable,

00:09:22.135 --> 00:09:24.370
it's like a mobile classroom.

00:09:24.370 --> 00:09:26.670
It's like one of those
families that has a school bus

00:09:26.670 --> 00:09:28.460
that just takes their kids around

00:09:28.460 --> 00:09:30.060
doing homework all the time."

00:09:30.060 --> 00:09:31.700
So, there'll be plenty of
opportunity for homework

00:09:31.700 --> 00:09:33.570
because I'm going to
drive, it's a rental car,

00:09:33.570 --> 00:09:35.690
you're not allowed to drive this thing

00:09:35.690 --> 00:09:37.410
so, you're going to be able
to do a lot of homework.

00:09:37.410 --> 00:09:38.243
He was like,

00:09:38.243 --> 00:09:39.437
"Yeah, okay, okay, yeah, all right."

00:09:41.330 --> 00:09:44.310
But this is like the
23rd or so, 22nd, 23rd

00:09:44.310 --> 00:09:46.601
when we made the call to
cancel the Moscow trip

00:09:46.601 --> 00:09:48.720
and decided to do this.

00:09:48.720 --> 00:09:50.420
So in terms of preparation,

00:09:50.420 --> 00:09:51.633
I actually, I let Jenna know

00:09:51.633 --> 00:09:53.730
that I was doing this
cross-country road trip

00:09:53.730 --> 00:09:54.563
and she was like,

00:09:54.563 --> 00:09:57.007
"Well, you should check out
the website roadtrippers.com."

00:09:58.092 --> 00:09:59.780
And I did, and that was actually,

00:09:59.780 --> 00:10:00.613
thank you for that, Jenna,

00:10:00.613 --> 00:10:03.400
that was very helpful
because what you end up doing

00:10:03.400 --> 00:10:06.150
is you basically go on there

00:10:06.150 --> 00:10:08.010
and you set your destination,

00:10:08.010 --> 00:10:10.500
your origin and your destination,

00:10:10.500 --> 00:10:15.300
and then it creates a
corridor around the route,

00:10:15.300 --> 00:10:19.303
like a 30 mile wide corridor,
which in my case was on 40,

00:10:19.303 --> 00:10:21.240
because I was going to take 40

00:10:21.240 --> 00:10:23.460
because I didn't want to just go.

00:10:23.460 --> 00:10:24.760
I wanted to get home,

00:10:24.760 --> 00:10:26.930
but I wanted to be able to
experience some stuff as well.

00:10:26.930 --> 00:10:27.990
But I didn't want to
do one of these things

00:10:27.990 --> 00:10:29.300
where you're like going up north.

00:10:29.300 --> 00:10:32.330
Also, winter weather was happening

00:10:32.330 --> 00:10:34.134
and I'll talk a little
bit more about that,

00:10:34.134 --> 00:10:36.360
we actually had a divert
our route because of that,

00:10:36.360 --> 00:10:39.270
but I wanted to stay 40 or south,

