00:52:51.210 --> 00:52:53.094
With like milk and yogurt and stuff.

00:52:53.094 --> 00:52:54.490
Gas station?

00:52:54.490 --> 00:52:56.550
Not a gas station, it is a restaurant.

00:52:56.550 --> 00:52:58.300
I've never been in a
place like this in my life

00:52:58.300 --> 00:52:59.957
where you've got the restaurant over here

00:52:59.957 --> 00:53:01.340
and the convenience store over here

00:53:01.340 --> 00:53:02.173
and it's all open,

00:53:02.173 --> 00:53:04.330
there's no dividers, and
there's like a cashier up there

00:53:04.330 --> 00:53:05.450
with a menu in the ice cream.

00:53:05.450 --> 00:53:06.950
Sounds like a truck stop to me.

00:53:06.950 --> 00:53:08.370
It was unlike anything I've ever,

00:53:08.370 --> 00:53:10.116
it was not any of those
things you're saying.

00:53:10.116 --> 00:53:11.320
And this is the chain?

00:53:11.320 --> 00:53:13.623
Yes, very popular chain.

00:53:14.960 --> 00:53:19.420
And shout out to the staff
of the Braum's and Amarillo,

00:53:19.420 --> 00:53:21.333
many of whom are Mythical Beasts.

00:53:22.980 --> 00:53:25.000
Did they give you a chocolate?

00:53:25.000 --> 00:53:25.898
Well, I ordered my food.

00:53:25.898 --> 00:53:27.710
I am not looking for the freebies

00:53:29.090 --> 00:53:29.970
Got to get those perks.

00:53:29.970 --> 00:53:31.850
Locke wanted a banana split.

00:53:31.850 --> 00:53:33.940
Again, he was like in
this Friday Night Lights,

00:53:33.940 --> 00:53:34.900
he's like, he can get a banana..

00:53:34.900 --> 00:53:36.280
So he got the banana split.

00:53:36.280 --> 00:53:37.737
And first of all, I was not hungry at all,

00:53:37.737 --> 00:53:39.980
but I got like a sundae
and ate the whole thing.

00:53:39.980 --> 00:53:41.600
Of course you did.

00:53:41.600 --> 00:53:43.663
I came to regret that the next day.

00:53:44.620 --> 00:53:47.240
But then, at this point,

00:53:47.240 --> 00:53:49.490
we're using hotel tonight
and like staying at

00:53:50.410 --> 00:53:52.071
the holiday Inn that's available.

00:53:52.071 --> 00:53:53.930
Last minute because I wasn't exactly sure

00:53:53.930 --> 00:53:55.530
whether we would make it.

00:53:55.530 --> 00:53:58.113
That night was not a
destination hotel, right?

00:53:59.660 --> 00:54:00.700
But we wake up in the morning

00:54:00.700 --> 00:54:02.380
and I don't know if you remember this,

00:54:02.380 --> 00:54:03.300
we stopped there before,

00:54:03.300 --> 00:54:06.730
but right outside of Amarillo
is the Cadillac Ranch,

00:54:06.730 --> 00:54:09.620
which again is basically a farmer

00:54:09.620 --> 00:54:12.470
who decided that he was
gonna just plant Cadillacs.

00:54:12.470 --> 00:54:13.861
I guess he was trying
to grow Cadillac trees.

00:54:13.861 --> 00:54:14.694
Oh, I've not been there,

00:54:14.694 --> 00:54:15.977
but I've seen potoes.

00:54:17.446 --> 00:54:18.313
You've seen potoes?

00:54:19.660 --> 00:54:21.050
It's weird that we didn't stop there

00:54:21.050 --> 00:54:22.779
because we literally drove past it.

00:54:22.779 --> 00:54:24.137
It's like I've read the word potoes

00:54:24.137 --> 00:54:24.970
and I've never heard it before.

00:54:24.970 --> 00:54:26.750
How do you say potoes?

00:54:26.750 --> 00:54:30.900
So we stopped and got some
potoes at the Cadillac Ranch.

00:54:30.900 --> 00:54:33.560
It was so cold that morning,

00:54:33.560 --> 00:54:35.760
but literally all it
is is like a corn field

00:54:35.760 --> 00:54:40.760
or the crops were not currently
planted and just Cadillacs.

00:54:40.870 --> 00:54:41.703
And of course...

00:54:41.703 --> 00:54:43.960
No entry fee?

00:54:43.960 --> 00:54:46.466
No entry fee and you can paint on them.

00:54:46.466 --> 00:54:47.310
Oh, you can?

00:54:47.310 --> 00:54:49.200
There's a bunch of spray paint bottles

00:54:49.200 --> 00:54:51.480
and people are constantly tagging them

00:54:51.480 --> 00:54:53.180
to the point they're like,

00:54:53.180 --> 00:54:55.390
the way that the pain
is dripping off of them.

00:54:55.390 --> 00:54:58.940
It's created a whole new
entity of a painted Cadillac.

00:54:58.940 --> 00:55:01.930
Very cool, great photo up or potoe up.

00:55:01.930 --> 00:55:05.510
We did that, but the thing
we were trying to get to

00:55:05.510 --> 00:55:07.350
that day was Albuquerque.

00:55:07.350 --> 00:55:09.840
How did you get to New Mexico?

00:55:09.840 --> 00:55:12.770
Again, I always have
like a food thing in mind

00:55:12.770 --> 00:55:14.590
and then I've got like a
sight seeing thing in mind.

00:55:14.590 --> 00:55:15.880
The food thing that I had in mind

00:55:15.880 --> 00:55:17.160
was getting some of that New Mexican,

00:55:17.160 --> 00:55:20.990
like green chili-type food,
which we stopped at a place,

00:55:20.990 --> 00:55:22.670
I can't remember the name
of it, but it was good.

00:55:22.670 --> 00:55:27.480
We got like the authentic
New Mexican cuisine,

00:55:27.480 --> 00:55:30.103
which is different than
just regular Mexican food.

00:55:30.103 --> 00:55:34.350
There's an emphasis on
the green chili sauce.

00:55:34.350 --> 00:55:37.260
But of course, Albuquerque
is known as the place

00:55:37.260 --> 00:55:38.600
where Breaking Bad was filmed,

00:55:38.600 --> 00:55:40.250
which is one of my
favorite shows of all time

00:55:40.250 --> 00:55:41.840
and one of Locke's
favorite shows of all time.

00:55:41.840 --> 00:55:42.990
We didn't watch it concurrently

00:55:42.990 --> 00:55:44.090
because I watched it a long time ago

00:55:44.090 --> 00:55:47.140
then he watched it on his
own like two years ago.

00:55:47.140 --> 00:55:48.664
So, there are multiple...

00:55:48.664 --> 00:55:50.207
They still film it there.

00:55:50.207 --> 00:55:53.590
They filmed, any exterior scene

00:55:53.590 --> 00:55:55.820
in Breaking Bad is in Albuquerque.

00:55:55.820 --> 00:55:59.650
The carwash, Walter White's
house, Jesse's house.

00:55:59.650 --> 00:56:00.618
Yeah, I remember the house.

00:56:00.618 --> 00:56:02.620
I guess the house was just like,

00:56:02.620 --> 00:56:04.110
I didn't know if it was a cheat,

00:56:04.110 --> 00:56:05.280
they shot there a lot, I guess.

00:56:05.280 --> 00:56:06.700
Lots of outside shots,

00:56:06.700 --> 00:56:08.130
places where they got
in fights on the road.

00:56:08.130 --> 00:56:10.280
So I think they either they shot,

00:56:10.280 --> 00:56:12.260
they shot a lot of it in Albuquerque.

00:56:12.260 --> 00:56:13.700
Like a lot of it.

00:56:13.700 --> 00:56:15.210
Not just exterior locations,

00:56:15.210 --> 00:56:16.460
there's lots of things going on there.

00:56:16.460 --> 00:56:18.510
So, were are you going to
do like a driving tour?

00:56:18.510 --> 00:56:20.310
Yeah, so there's multiple sites

00:56:20.310 --> 00:56:22.570
that have put together the
different stops and like,

00:56:22.570 --> 00:56:24.530
okay, this part of town
you can see these things,

00:56:24.530 --> 00:56:26.440
this part of town you
can see these things.

00:56:26.440 --> 00:56:29.306
Of course, the thing that we
were most excited about was,

00:56:29.306 --> 00:56:32.170
Walter and Skyler's house, the main house.

00:56:32.170 --> 00:56:33.003
Yeah.

00:56:33.003 --> 00:56:35.310
Now, I kind of heard that,

00:56:35.310 --> 00:56:36.620
because none of this is official

00:56:36.620 --> 00:56:38.240
and this is just somebody's
private residence

00:56:38.240 --> 00:56:39.500
that the people who live there

00:56:39.500 --> 00:56:40.837
are not exactly thrilled about this.

00:56:40.837 --> 00:56:44.017
And in fact, in the thing
that I looked up, it said,

00:56:44.017 --> 00:56:46.010
"The people who live here are not happy

00:56:46.010 --> 00:56:50.850
about the fact that they
own this historic house.

00:56:50.850 --> 00:56:53.420
And they kind of want
you to take your pictures

00:56:53.420 --> 00:56:55.330
from across the street
and get out of there."

00:56:55.330 --> 00:56:56.720
So I was like, "Oh, okay, that's fine.

00:56:56.720 --> 00:56:58.640
I'll just take my picture
from across the street

00:56:58.640 --> 00:56:59.473
and get out of there."

00:56:59.473 --> 00:57:02.250
Just annoy the neighbors by
being in front of their house.

00:57:02.250 --> 00:57:03.640
Well, I mean, how annoying is it

00:57:03.640 --> 00:57:04.830
for somebody to stop for a second?

00:57:04.830 --> 00:57:05.932
If there's a constant stream, I get it.

00:57:05.932 --> 00:57:08.440
Well, let me tell you,

00:57:08.440 --> 00:57:10.570
saying that they're not pleased with it

00:57:10.570 --> 00:57:12.320
or they don't think it is ideal

00:57:12.320 --> 00:57:15.460
is the understatement of the year, okay?

00:57:15.460 --> 00:57:17.330
We pull up to the house.

00:57:17.330 --> 00:57:18.190
The first thing I noticed...

00:57:18.190 --> 00:57:19.170
It's in the neighborhood, right?

00:57:19.170 --> 00:57:21.660
It's in a neighborhood,
it's just like a suburb.

00:57:21.660 --> 00:57:25.550
There is a fence that has
been erected around the house

00:57:25.550 --> 00:57:27.670
that completely changes the look of it

00:57:27.670 --> 00:57:29.170
and changes the view somewhat.

00:57:29.170 --> 00:57:30.333
Chain like fence?

00:57:31.449 --> 00:57:34.700
There's a picture, I can't remember.

00:57:34.700 --> 00:57:36.900
It's not a chain-link kind of thing,

00:57:36.900 --> 00:57:38.394
but you can kind of see through it,

00:57:38.394 --> 00:57:40.950
but it doesn't look the same.

00:57:40.950 --> 00:57:43.860
And then there's multiple
signs on the fence

00:57:43.860 --> 00:57:48.120
and on the house that says,
this is a private residence,

00:57:48.120 --> 00:57:50.740
take your pictures from
across the street and move on

00:57:50.740 --> 00:57:51.790
or something like that.

00:57:51.790 --> 00:57:54.560
So, we park across the
street and then we get out.

00:57:54.560 --> 00:57:57.510
And then I see that there is an older lady

00:57:57.510 --> 00:58:00.220
who comes out of the front door

00:58:00.220 --> 00:58:01.053
Of the house?

00:58:01.053 --> 00:58:02.234
of the house.

00:58:03.320 --> 00:58:07.970
And she just looks at me,
and this woman looks mad

00:58:07.970 --> 00:58:10.290
and she just stands there
looking at me like this.

00:58:10.290 --> 00:58:12.230
Crosses her arms, sets up,

00:58:12.230 --> 00:58:13.530
and just makes eye contact with me.

00:58:13.530 --> 00:58:15.410
And I'm thinking like, okay,

00:58:15.410 --> 00:58:16.550
and I'm like, I've got to say something.

00:58:16.550 --> 00:58:18.270
I'm like, "Hey, how you doing?

00:58:18.270 --> 00:58:20.510
You mind if we get a picture?"

00:58:20.510 --> 00:58:24.200
And she said, "I do mind,
this is a private residence.

00:58:24.200 --> 00:58:26.020
It has always been a private residence.

00:58:26.020 --> 00:58:28.240
And it will always be
a private residence."

00:58:28.240 --> 00:58:30.297
What, all these years later,

00:58:30.297 --> 00:58:34.700
the owner is having a
face-to-face interaction

00:58:34.700 --> 00:58:36.630
with you about it and it's this heated

00:58:36.630 --> 00:58:38.050
after all of this time?

00:58:38.050 --> 00:58:42.710
Her, I don't know when she
bought the house pre or post,

00:58:42.710 --> 00:58:45.270
but the thing is, listen, at that point

00:58:45.270 --> 00:58:47.750
I felt so sorry for her

00:58:47.750 --> 00:58:49.990
and the level of bitterness

00:58:49.990 --> 00:58:53.000
that is consuming her and her life

00:58:53.000 --> 00:58:55.010
that I was like, "Okay, no problem."

00:58:55.010 --> 00:58:56.660
And instead of like being an asshole,

00:58:56.660 --> 00:58:58.530
and like taking a picture
or talking back to her,

00:58:58.530 --> 00:59:00.030
which I'm sure a lot of people do,

00:59:00.030 --> 00:59:00.863
I was just like, "Okay."

00:59:00.863 --> 00:59:04.267
And we got back in the
car and Locke was like,

00:59:04.267 --> 00:59:05.670
"Dad, I told you that they didn't like."

00:59:05.670 --> 00:59:07.120
I was like, "You didn't tell me

00:59:07.120 --> 00:59:08.840
that it was this serious of a thing."

00:59:08.840 --> 00:59:10.130
He was like, "No, no, Micah,

00:59:10.130 --> 00:59:11.627
My nephew and his cousin.

00:59:11.627 --> 00:59:12.460
"Micah said, she really hates it."

00:59:12.460 --> 00:59:14.880
I was like, "You didn't tell me that."

00:59:14.880 --> 00:59:16.600
Your nephews had visited it?

00:59:16.600 --> 00:59:17.720
No, they just knew

00:59:17.720 --> 00:59:19.350
because Locke said that
we were going to do it

00:59:19.350 --> 00:59:22.100
and they like were talking,
looked it up online.

00:59:22.100 --> 00:59:23.550
And I was just like,

00:59:23.550 --> 00:59:26.530
man, I couldn't wrap my
mind around the like,

00:59:26.530 --> 00:59:29.210
did this woman buy this house

00:59:29.210 --> 00:59:31.530
and then let them film there for a fee,

00:59:31.530 --> 00:59:33.030
never expecting that it would become

00:59:33.030 --> 00:59:34.910
this huge cultural phenomenon

00:59:34.910 --> 00:59:36.820
and now she just doesn't sell the house

00:59:36.820 --> 00:59:39.050
or did she buy the house after the fact

00:59:39.050 --> 00:59:42.780
and didn't fully know how popular it was?

00:59:42.780 --> 00:59:43.990
I mean, all these years later,

00:59:43.990 --> 00:59:45.810
I mean, I'm sure every year that passes

00:59:45.810 --> 00:59:47.860
less and less people visit.

00:59:47.860 --> 00:59:50.163
But as we were leaving, there
was another person coming in.

00:59:50.163 --> 00:59:50.996
There was?

00:59:50.996 --> 00:59:53.980
Oh yeah, it is a constant
stream of traffic.

00:59:53.980 --> 00:59:55.578
So I can only imagine
how the neighbors feel.

00:59:55.578 --> 00:59:57.990
I don't feel great about
the fact that we did it,

00:59:57.990 --> 01:00:01.960
but we were not intrusive.

01:00:01.960 --> 01:00:03.070
And if she hadn't said anything,

01:00:03.070 --> 01:00:04.040
we would just gotten out of the car

01:00:04.040 --> 01:00:04.873
in the middle of the road,

01:00:04.873 --> 01:00:06.320
taken a picture and just gone on.

01:00:06.320 --> 01:00:08.630
An annoyance, yes,

01:00:08.630 --> 01:00:09.700
so, I don't really know what the way,

01:00:09.700 --> 01:00:10.780
if you're going to be annoyed by it

01:00:10.780 --> 01:00:11.880
I think you have to sell the house

01:00:11.880 --> 01:00:13.640
to someone who would think it was cool

01:00:13.640 --> 01:00:17.350
or like Vince Gilligan
should just buy the house

01:00:17.350 --> 01:00:20.280
from this woman and let people take

01:00:20.280 --> 01:00:21.113
all the pictures they want,

01:00:21.113 --> 01:00:22.640
I don't know, I don't
know what the solution is.

01:00:22.640 --> 01:00:26.098
Yeah, you've ruined this
person's life unintentionally

01:00:26.098 --> 01:00:29.120
and then they can't sell it at a price

01:00:29.120 --> 01:00:32.450
because of what it is that
they would need to sell it for,

01:00:32.450 --> 01:00:33.559
so they just live there.

01:00:33.559 --> 01:00:34.392
Well, that's the thing,

01:00:34.392 --> 01:00:35.357
Locke was like, "Wouldn't
the value go up?"

01:00:35.357 --> 01:00:37.190
I was like, "No, think about it,

01:00:37.190 --> 01:00:40.630
whenever the number of
people who are interested

01:00:40.630 --> 01:00:41.963
in a place goes down,

01:00:44.000 --> 01:00:46.680
more people would be
turned off by the idea

01:00:46.680 --> 01:00:48.390
of buying a landmark that
people would come to.

01:00:48.390 --> 01:00:50.170
So if you lower the amount of people

01:00:50.170 --> 01:00:51.080
who are demanding of the house,

01:00:51.080 --> 01:00:53.090
then the price of the house
goes down, ironically."

01:00:53.090 --> 01:00:55.406
So Lance and Lacey should buy it.

01:00:57.087 --> 01:00:57.920
and turn it into an Airbnb.

01:00:57.920 --> 01:00:58.753
To a hotel.

01:00:58.753 --> 01:00:59.970
So, I don't know what the history is,

01:00:59.970 --> 01:01:00.803
but I felt bad about it.

01:01:00.803 --> 01:01:02.130
Well, the rest of the neighborhood

01:01:02.130 --> 01:01:03.850
probably won't let them
turn it into an Airbnb,

01:01:03.850 --> 01:01:06.268
but that's what should have happened.

01:01:06.268 --> 01:01:09.180
It should be a freaking,
put Lance and Lacey on it.

01:01:09.180 --> 01:01:10.840
But that was just the
beginning of the tour,

01:01:10.840 --> 01:01:13.390
because the next stop was the carwash,

01:01:13.390 --> 01:01:15.660
which I can't remember the name
of the carwash in the show,

01:01:15.660 --> 01:01:16.752
but this is where the carwash

01:01:16.752 --> 01:01:19.600
that Walt buys to launder the money.

01:01:19.600 --> 01:01:22.720
And it is still intact and
still functioning as a carwash.

01:01:22.720 --> 01:01:26.020
And it's just very iconic shape.

01:01:26.020 --> 01:01:29.110
So, we pull up to the carwash

01:01:29.110 --> 01:01:31.700
and there's a giant open
parking lot right next to it,

01:01:31.700 --> 01:01:33.830
which makes it easy to kind of
stop and just get a picture.

01:01:33.830 --> 01:01:35.410
And like the guys watching the cars

01:01:35.410 --> 01:01:36.243
kind of look at you like,

01:01:36.243 --> 01:01:38.150
"Yeah, this happens all day every day."

01:01:38.150 --> 01:01:39.500
But it's the place of business.

01:01:39.500 --> 01:01:40.620
Yeah, it's not a big deal.

01:01:40.620 --> 01:01:41.530
They don't care probably.

01:01:41.530 --> 01:01:45.190
There's a group of people
who had just taken pictures

01:01:45.190 --> 01:01:46.600
in front of the carwash

01:01:46.600 --> 01:01:48.550
who were also on the self-guided tour,

01:01:48.550 --> 01:01:50.280
they're getting back into their car

01:01:50.280 --> 01:01:51.960
and then Locke and I take a picture

01:01:51.960 --> 01:01:53.910
and like take a little video out there

01:01:53.910 --> 01:01:55.990
and then we see them get
back out of their car

01:01:55.990 --> 01:01:58.377
and there's a girl who
has this look on her face.

01:01:58.377 --> 01:02:01.037
I'm like this, "She must
be a giant Breaking Bad fan

01:02:01.037 --> 01:02:03.450
because she's coming back for a more."

01:02:03.450 --> 01:02:08.450
It turns out that she was
a huge Mythical Beast,

01:02:08.510 --> 01:02:10.303
and it was crazy, she...

01:02:10.303 --> 01:02:12.517
Did you tell her, "Don't
go to Walt's house."

01:02:12.517 --> 01:02:15.530
I did, I said, we just had
an interaction with a lady,

01:02:15.530 --> 01:02:17.350
because they had not been there yet,

01:02:17.350 --> 01:02:18.680
it was their next stop.

01:02:18.680 --> 01:02:21.130
I don't know if they went, but yeah.

01:02:21.130 --> 01:02:23.320
So I can't remember your name.

01:02:23.320 --> 01:02:25.097
You tweeted at me after the fact,

01:02:25.097 --> 01:02:26.560
and I'm sorry, I can't remember your name,

01:02:26.560 --> 01:02:28.083
but you know who you are.

01:02:28.083 --> 01:02:30.943
We got some pictures out
in front of the carwash,

01:02:33.050 --> 01:02:35.440
and then we continued on to...

01:02:35.440 --> 01:02:36.480
They should give you a chocolate.

01:02:36.480 --> 01:02:39.400
Jesse Pinkman's house,
which apparently they're not

01:02:39.400 --> 01:02:41.069
too keen on it, but they weren't there

01:02:41.069 --> 01:02:43.020
and there's no signs.

01:02:43.020 --> 01:02:45.630
And again, as we stopped to take a picture

01:02:45.630 --> 01:02:48.720
another father and son in another car

01:02:48.720 --> 01:02:49.871
were stopping as we left.

01:02:49.871 --> 01:02:51.960
It's a constant stream

01:02:51.960 --> 01:02:53.950
because Breaking Bad
is such an iconic show,

01:02:53.950 --> 01:02:56.260
and so many points of reference for it.

01:02:56.260 --> 01:02:58.400
I mean, we've been to
the Brady Bunch house

01:02:58.400 --> 01:02:59.780
and Toluca Lake...

01:02:59.780 --> 01:03:02.996
Well, in LA there's
houses all over the place

01:03:02.996 --> 01:03:03.829
that you can go.

01:03:03.829 --> 01:03:08.100
Like, the Jamie Lee Curtis'

01:03:08.100 --> 01:03:11.350
Halloween house, Brady took me there.

01:03:11.350 --> 01:03:15.220
And they have a little sign
that says take your picture,

01:03:15.220 --> 01:03:16.620
but keep your distance.

01:03:16.620 --> 01:03:17.453
Yeah.

01:03:17.453 --> 01:03:19.920
Actually, they had candy on their porch

01:03:19.920 --> 01:03:21.409
and they were like,

01:03:21.409 --> 01:03:24.020
"Feel free to take some
candy, take a picture,

01:03:24.020 --> 01:03:26.090
and thank you for being nice about this."

01:03:26.090 --> 01:03:30.600
Yeah, to me, it's like the
main difference is like,

01:03:30.600 --> 01:03:34.010
if you live in New York City
and you live in the city,

01:03:34.010 --> 01:03:35.440
well, there's all kinds of stuff

01:03:35.440 --> 01:03:37.660
going on right outside
your front door constantly.

01:03:37.660 --> 01:03:38.840
It's a different lifestyle

01:03:38.840 --> 01:03:40.727
than what you would get
in suburban Albuquerque.

01:03:40.727 --> 01:03:43.420
But that would be my
way of dealing with it,

01:03:43.420 --> 01:03:44.540
would be like, "Hey, listen,

01:03:44.540 --> 01:03:46.150
people are going to be
constantly coming to our house

01:03:46.150 --> 01:03:47.204
and taking pictures, just to make sure

01:03:47.204 --> 01:03:49.200
they can't see in the windows

01:03:49.200 --> 01:03:51.420
and then if they come
by, we can greet them"

01:03:51.420 --> 01:03:53.330
It's tough, it's tough man.

01:03:53.330 --> 01:03:54.581
But, I didn't sign up for it.

01:03:54.581 --> 01:03:57.080
I didn't let him film there
and I didn't buy the house.

01:03:57.080 --> 01:03:59.610
Okay. So that was Albuquerque.

01:03:59.610 --> 01:04:01.600
The next stop was supposed to be,

01:04:01.600 --> 01:04:04.640
we were going to keep going
west and then go up north

01:04:04.640 --> 01:04:05.473
to the Grand Canyon.

01:04:05.473 --> 01:04:07.600
We were actually going
to go to Monument Valley

01:04:07.600 --> 01:04:12.397
that very famous drive
in, I guess it's Arizona,

01:04:14.710 --> 01:04:16.810
New Mexico or Arizona, but it's like,

01:04:16.810 --> 01:04:17.643
you've seen it a million times.

01:04:17.643 --> 01:04:19.940
One of the most iconic
drives in all of America.

01:04:19.940 --> 01:04:21.470
Then there was a horseshoe.

01:04:21.470 --> 01:04:23.863
Ben, which is that crazy
horse you've seen on Instagram

01:04:23.863 --> 01:04:25.550
1000 times and then the Grand Canyon.

01:04:25.550 --> 01:04:28.653
But the winter weather was
really rolling in to the tune of,

01:04:29.960 --> 01:04:33.500
it was going to be a high of like 18°

01:04:33.500 --> 01:04:35.453
at the Grand Canyon and snowing.

01:04:36.290 --> 01:04:38.820
And I'm in a expedition,
it's not four wheel drive.

01:04:38.820 --> 01:04:40.810
Oh, it's not, okay.

01:04:40.810 --> 01:04:43.370
So I was like, the idea
of getting up there

01:04:43.370 --> 01:04:45.150
and trying to drive through this snow

01:04:45.150 --> 01:04:46.930
and also it being so cold,

01:04:46.930 --> 01:04:48.350
I just said, we've got to pull an audible,

01:04:48.350 --> 01:04:49.560
we've got to go south.

01:04:49.560 --> 01:04:50.800
We're going to miss the Grand Canyon,

01:04:50.800 --> 01:04:53.370
this is going to be
another time and place.

01:04:53.370 --> 01:04:55.040
Another time, same place,

01:04:55.040 --> 01:04:56.600
that we will see the Grand Canyon

01:04:56.600 --> 01:04:58.000
and all this other stuff.

01:04:58.000 --> 01:05:00.750
So I was scrambling that night,

01:05:00.750 --> 01:05:03.210
it was actually the night
we stayed in Amarillo,

01:05:03.210 --> 01:05:04.330
I was trying to figure out like,

01:05:04.330 --> 01:05:05.700
okay, after we stopped in Albuquerque,

01:05:05.700 --> 01:05:06.533
where are we going.

01:05:06.533 --> 01:05:07.366
Phoenix?

01:05:07.366 --> 01:05:08.880
Well, what I determined was

01:05:08.880 --> 01:05:10.300
is there were two really cool things

01:05:10.300 --> 01:05:13.050
that I wanted to see if we were
to go south towards Tucson,

01:05:13.050 --> 01:05:14.380
and yes, and then in the Phoenix.

01:05:14.380 --> 01:05:15.510
Okay.

01:05:15.510 --> 01:05:19.197
The first, if you head
south of Albuquerque,

01:05:19.197 --> 01:05:21.350
if you're heading towards Tucson,

01:05:21.350 --> 01:05:22.710
you can kind of divert off and kind of

01:05:22.710 --> 01:05:25.260
go through some crazy wilderness

01:05:25.260 --> 01:05:28.570
and see something called
the Very Large Array.

01:05:28.570 --> 01:05:29.700
Now, I don't know if you remember,

01:05:29.700 --> 01:05:32.470
but when we made the SETI song

01:05:32.470 --> 01:05:35.160
and we had like stock
pictures of these satellites

01:05:35.160 --> 01:05:37.030
that are just all giant satellites,

01:05:37.030 --> 01:05:38.770
like pointing towards the sky,

01:05:38.770 --> 01:05:40.420
that's the Very Large Array.

01:05:40.420 --> 01:05:43.455
It's the largest array
of radio telescopes.

01:05:43.455 --> 01:05:47.634
I think in, definitely North
America, maybe the world.

01:05:47.634 --> 01:05:51.660
I said it's 22 miles across
and Locke thought I meant

01:05:51.660 --> 01:05:53.335
one satellite that was 22 miles across.

01:05:53.335 --> 01:05:56.453
So he was a little bit disappointed
when I showed up there.

01:05:57.841 --> 01:06:01.000
It's just 22 miles across
a field, essentially,

01:06:01.000 --> 01:06:03.170
of these giant, once you
get right up next to them,

01:06:03.170 --> 01:06:06.670
these giant telescopes that are making

01:06:06.670 --> 01:06:08.050
all these incredible discoveries of...

01:06:08.050 --> 01:06:09.960
You can drive through them?

01:06:09.960 --> 01:06:12.830
Well, COVID had closed
down the visitor center,

01:06:12.830 --> 01:06:16.000
which also includes a self-guided tour

01:06:16.000 --> 01:06:19.463
where you can walk directly
up to one without trespassing.

01:06:20.360 --> 01:06:22.120
We did walk pretty much up to one

01:06:22.120 --> 01:06:24.083
with a very minor trespassing.

01:06:25.040 --> 01:06:26.360
Because it's just as middle of this field,

01:06:26.360 --> 01:06:27.900
literally there's cows all around them.

01:06:27.900 --> 01:06:29.754
I mean, there's like
cattle grazing out there.

01:06:29.754 --> 01:06:32.671
That drive out there is so incredible,

01:06:32.671 --> 01:06:35.970
that part of the country is
just beautiful and desolate,

01:06:35.970 --> 01:06:37.707
and, I mean, you drive and
drive and drive and drive

01:06:37.707 --> 01:06:41.200
and you find like, oh,
there's giant satellites

01:06:41.200 --> 01:06:43.880
on these like basically railroad tracks.

01:06:43.880 --> 01:06:45.850
It's how they kind of move them around

01:06:45.850 --> 01:06:46.683
or like do maintenance on them

01:06:46.683 --> 01:06:47.953
because there's railroad tracks

01:06:47.953 --> 01:06:48.786
again next to it.

01:06:48.786 --> 01:06:50.500
It's all operable, they're
still listening for aliens.

01:06:50.500 --> 01:06:55.280
Okay, so I thought that the
whole purpose of the thing

01:06:55.280 --> 01:06:56.300
was listening for aliens,

01:06:56.300 --> 01:06:58.863
but no, it's a government program where,

01:07:00.512 --> 01:07:02.410
it might be private.

01:07:02.410 --> 01:07:04.550
The government is
involved, NASA is involved,

01:07:04.550 --> 01:07:07.760
because they're using radio telescopes

01:07:07.760 --> 01:07:09.830
to discover black holes

01:07:09.830 --> 01:07:11.610
and all this information about the galaxy.

01:07:11.610 --> 01:07:14.430
So they're constantly scanning the skies,

01:07:14.430 --> 01:07:16.118
using radio frequencies,

01:07:16.118 --> 01:07:19.110
essentially that's how
radio telescopes work,

01:07:19.110 --> 01:07:20.720
mapping and getting pictures of things

01:07:20.720 --> 01:07:22.056
using radio frequencies.

01:07:22.056 --> 01:07:23.570
And they're incredibly sensitive

01:07:23.570 --> 01:07:25.010
and there's a bunch of them.

01:07:25.010 --> 01:07:27.758
But SETI, the Search
for Extra Terrestrial,

01:07:27.758 --> 01:07:30.710
whatever, that thing that people who are

01:07:30.710 --> 01:07:35.040
listening for extra terrestrial
life out in the cosmos,

01:07:35.040 --> 01:07:36.677
they've partnered with them

01:07:36.677 --> 01:07:40.190
and so they basically have
access to the stream of data

01:07:40.190 --> 01:07:42.800
that all these satellites are gathering.

01:07:42.800 --> 01:07:43.810
But it's pretty crazy.

01:07:43.810 --> 01:07:47.510
I wish we could have
gone in to the gift shop

01:07:47.510 --> 01:07:48.377
and gotten some souvenirs,

01:07:48.377 --> 01:07:50.880
but we just got some cool pictures.

01:07:50.880 --> 01:07:52.480
That was pretty much all we got.

01:07:53.800 --> 01:07:58.180
But the goal was to get to a
place called Globe Arizona,

01:07:58.180 --> 01:08:01.476
which is in the mountains basically.

01:08:01.476 --> 01:08:04.103
Arizona has quite a
landscape, I don't know...

01:08:04.103 --> 01:08:06.437
I've never heard of globe.

01:08:06.437 --> 01:08:07.810
There's no reason to,

01:08:07.810 --> 01:08:10.218
it was just a spot that kind
of made sense for us to get to,

01:08:10.218 --> 01:08:14.130
to then be able to go to
our next stop the next day.

01:08:14.130 --> 01:08:17.370
But the problem with this
is even though I had,

01:08:17.370 --> 01:08:19.620
I was so thankful that we had gone south,

01:08:19.620 --> 01:08:21.410
but going through the mountains,

01:08:21.410 --> 01:08:23.800
the elevation got up to
like five or 6,000 feet

01:08:23.800 --> 01:08:26.920
and we went through like
blizzard conditions.

01:08:26.920 --> 01:08:27.753
Really?

01:08:27.753 --> 01:08:30.440
Multiple times at night
trying to get to Globe.

01:08:30.440 --> 01:08:33.416
And I was like, we stopped and eight

01:08:33.416 --> 01:08:35.900
in this middle of nowhere town

01:08:35.900 --> 01:08:38.092
and then I'm like looking at
the elevation of where we're at

01:08:38.092 --> 01:08:40.610
and elevation of where we
need to get and it's down.

01:08:40.610 --> 01:08:42.010
So I'm like, okay, well
it's not going to be

01:08:42.010 --> 01:08:43.180
any worse than it is right now.

01:08:43.180 --> 01:08:44.450
I didn't know that it
was going to require me

01:08:44.450 --> 01:08:46.490
going up higher and then,

01:08:46.490 --> 01:08:47.640
so there was like multiple times

01:08:47.640 --> 01:08:49.720
where I kind of got to snow,

01:08:49.720 --> 01:08:54.040
just like blinding snow,
down the side of a mountain

01:08:54.040 --> 01:08:55.938
and I'm freaking out, right?

01:08:55.938 --> 01:08:58.460
And Locke is like , "Dad, just relax."

01:08:58.460 --> 01:09:00.240
I'm like, "I don't want to get stuck."

01:09:00.240 --> 01:09:01.910
Are there other cars driving in this?

01:09:01.910 --> 01:09:04.360
Very few, that kept scaring me too.

01:09:04.360 --> 01:09:05.760
There's just very few other people

01:09:05.760 --> 01:09:06.997
who've made a decision to be on the road.

01:09:06.997 --> 01:09:08.360
And so I'm going pretty slow.

01:09:08.360 --> 01:09:09.820
It took us a while.

01:09:09.820 --> 01:09:13.630
We finally get low enough where
we start seeing some cactus

01:09:13.630 --> 01:09:16.450
and we're outside, it's all dark,

01:09:16.450 --> 01:09:18.620
I can kind of see them with my headlights,

01:09:18.620 --> 01:09:20.290
but we stop and spend
the night in Globe again,

01:09:20.290 --> 01:09:21.570
just like, we're just going with

01:09:21.570 --> 01:09:23.840
whatever's open at that
point, just some hotel.

01:09:23.840 --> 01:09:25.430
You're just ramblers at this point?

01:09:25.430 --> 01:09:26.810
It's all blurring together.

01:09:26.810 --> 01:09:30.343
But, we were headed for Biosphere 2.

01:09:33.985 --> 01:09:36.160
I've got the T-shirt to proof it.

01:09:36.160 --> 01:09:40.420
Biosphere 2, I've watched a documentary

01:09:40.420 --> 01:09:42.250
all about Biosphere 2.

01:09:42.250 --> 01:09:44.230
And we talked about it
pretty extensively on GMM

01:09:44.230 --> 01:09:45.450
a couple of years ago as well.

01:09:45.450 --> 01:09:47.160
Oh, I didn't, okay,
because I've watched this

01:09:47.160 --> 01:09:48.390
much more recently.

01:09:48.390 --> 01:09:50.620
It's been years since we
talked about it at GMM.

01:09:50.620 --> 01:09:54.580
Because it started in
the '70s, maybe earlier.

01:09:54.580 --> 01:09:55.860
No, it was in the '90s.

01:09:55.860 --> 01:09:57.075
It was the '90s?

01:09:57.075 --> 01:09:58.240
Well-

01:09:58.240 --> 01:09:59.073
No.

01:09:59.073 --> 01:10:01.300
It was maybe the late '80s, early '90s.

01:10:01.300 --> 01:10:04.000
Definitely, they were wearing
like eighties clothes.

01:10:04.000 --> 01:10:06.996
I thought it was '70s, definitely '80s.

01:10:06.996 --> 01:10:08.510
It was definitely '80s.

01:10:08.510 --> 01:10:09.363
It wasn't any earlier than that.

01:10:09.363 --> 01:10:12.730
I mean, they may have started
research earlier than that.

01:10:12.730 --> 01:10:16.320
But I remember this from when I was a kid.

01:10:16.320 --> 01:10:19.720
It's a private-funded biosphere,

01:10:19.720 --> 01:10:23.840
self-contained human experiment of,

01:10:23.840 --> 01:10:28.350
well, an experiment of sending
these humans into this,

01:10:28.350 --> 01:10:30.320
a team, it's kind of like astronauts,

01:10:30.320 --> 01:10:31.960
but they're going into his biosphere.

01:10:31.960 --> 01:10:34.820
And completely self-contained, so...

01:10:34.820 --> 01:10:37.270
And certifying that nothing's
going to go in or out.

01:10:37.270 --> 01:10:38.222
Including the air.

01:10:38.222 --> 01:10:40.650
So this was basically an experiment

01:10:40.650 --> 01:10:42.244
to see if human life can be sustained

01:10:42.244 --> 01:10:43.360
in one of these things.

01:10:43.360 --> 01:10:46.690
And the applications
could be on another planet

01:10:46.690 --> 01:10:48.900
or at the end of the
world, that kind of thing.

01:10:48.900 --> 01:10:52.590
So, the atmosphere was
completely self-contained.

01:10:52.590 --> 01:10:56.011
The water that they used,
completely self-contained,

01:10:56.011 --> 01:10:58.070
they grew their own food,

01:10:58.070 --> 01:11:01.060
nothing, for two years,
nothing could come in or out.

01:11:01.060 --> 01:11:02.530
Except for the breaches.

01:11:02.530 --> 01:11:05.167
Then the press jumped all
over, which is part of the,

01:11:05.167 --> 01:11:07.220
the documentary is pretty decent,

01:11:07.220 --> 01:11:09.520
I watched it on a plane.

01:11:09.520 --> 01:11:14.100
So, knowing a little
bit about the history,

01:11:14.100 --> 01:11:16.187
I was like, "Who owns it?"

01:11:16.187 --> 01:11:17.967
"Oh, the university of
Arizona owns it now."

01:11:17.967 --> 01:11:20.886
I'm like, I wonder if they've
like completely covered,

01:11:20.886 --> 01:11:23.400
it says they're using it
actively for research now.

01:11:23.400 --> 01:11:24.444
So I was just like,

01:11:24.444 --> 01:11:26.100
is this going to be a place that

01:11:26.100 --> 01:11:28.380
is embarrassed by the
history of Biosphere?

01:11:28.380 --> 01:11:30.610
Because it was kind of a big embarrassment

01:11:30.610 --> 01:11:33.470
in a lot of ways, and a
failure in a lot of ways.

01:11:33.470 --> 01:11:34.897
Are they going to be embarrassed by it

01:11:34.897 --> 01:11:37.790
or are they still like
associated with the same people

01:11:37.790 --> 01:11:38.990
and so they're like glossing over

01:11:38.990 --> 01:11:41.170
and trying to make it seem
like it was better than it was?

01:11:41.170 --> 01:11:44.530
No, they fully embrace
exactly what happened

01:11:44.530 --> 01:11:46.940
and they've left a lot of it intact,

01:11:46.940 --> 01:11:48.800
including like this is an apartment

01:11:48.800 --> 01:11:49.970
where one of the people lived

01:11:49.970 --> 01:11:51.170
and you can look inside and see

01:11:51.170 --> 01:11:53.750
this is the kitchen,
it's exactly how it was.

01:11:53.750 --> 01:11:56.260
So you took a tour inside?

01:11:56.260 --> 01:11:58.440
Yes, I went in every part of it.

01:11:58.440 --> 01:12:00.440
Oh, how long does that take?

01:12:00.440 --> 01:12:02.090
It took us about an hour and a half

01:12:02.090 --> 01:12:05.240
and we're moving pretty fast.

01:12:05.240 --> 01:12:06.287
Okay, that's pretty cool.

01:12:06.287 --> 01:12:07.862
But there's like a rain forest.

01:12:07.862 --> 01:12:11.760
You could go outside
and you could spectate,

01:12:11.760 --> 01:12:14.010
like you did the Breaking Bad house

01:12:14.010 --> 01:12:15.560
and like look through the glass.

01:12:15.560 --> 01:12:17.640
and the scientist's working and stuff,

01:12:17.640 --> 01:12:19.130
but of course you couldn't go inside

01:12:19.130 --> 01:12:22.270
because there were no breaches.

01:12:22.270 --> 01:12:23.627
But this was one of the
highlights of the trip.

01:12:23.627 --> 01:12:26.546
That cool, because it's huge, right?

01:12:26.546 --> 01:12:29.710
It looks huge, like a Puig greenhouse.

01:12:29.710 --> 01:12:32.210
Inside is four acres,

01:12:32.210 --> 01:12:34.620
which if you can imagine
a house that's four acres,

01:12:34.620 --> 01:12:38.170
that's a giant house. People don't...

01:12:38.170 --> 01:12:39.900
That's smaller than I thought though.

01:12:39.900 --> 01:12:43.880
Well, but if you think about most lots,

01:12:43.880 --> 01:12:47.610
if you have an acre of
land in Los Angeles,

01:12:47.610 --> 01:12:50.640
that's like 10 times bigger
than everybody else's house.

01:12:50.640 --> 01:12:51.473
Oh, sure.

01:12:51.473 --> 01:12:52.850
And then if the whole thing
is covered with a house

01:12:52.850 --> 01:12:54.483
and you do that times four,

01:12:55.609 --> 01:12:59.410
it's massive, once you get inside.

01:12:59.410 --> 01:13:02.257
And it's all still working
and all still functional.

01:13:02.257 --> 01:13:05.620
They have an indoor ocean,

01:13:05.620 --> 01:13:08.360
which at the time they had gone

01:13:08.360 --> 01:13:09.830
and collected coral and stuff

01:13:09.830 --> 01:13:12.850
and created an active
quote coral reef in there.

01:13:12.850 --> 01:13:14.290
The coral reef has died

01:13:14.290 --> 01:13:17.710
because at some point
Columbia University owned it

01:13:17.710 --> 01:13:18.980
before University of Arizona

01:13:18.980 --> 01:13:20.850
and they kind of let
the core coral reef die

01:13:20.850 --> 01:13:21.683
for whatever reason,

01:13:21.683 --> 01:13:23.360
but it's still there
actively studying some things

01:13:23.360 --> 01:13:24.193
and they're actually going to try

01:13:24.193 --> 01:13:25.560
to make it a coral reef again.

01:13:26.429 --> 01:13:29.860
There's just a number of
like scientific elements

01:13:29.860 --> 01:13:32.920
of like the way that the
energy was self-contained

01:13:32.920 --> 01:13:34.100
and then there was this giant,

01:13:34.100 --> 01:13:35.790
if you see the picture of Biosphere,

01:13:35.790 --> 01:13:38.100
there's these giant domes.

01:13:38.100 --> 01:13:39.090
You can't go inside those,

01:13:39.090 --> 01:13:41.950
but those are basically giant diaphragms,

01:13:41.950 --> 01:13:44.370
they're called lungs, to allow,

01:13:44.370 --> 01:13:46.860
when the air pressure increases

01:13:46.860 --> 01:13:50.050
because of temperature
changes, it expands.

01:13:50.050 --> 01:13:51.656
And like it's like a bubble that fills up

01:13:51.656 --> 01:13:53.610
to allow the pressure of the air

01:13:53.610 --> 01:13:56.290
to not bust the windows out.

01:13:56.290 --> 01:13:58.210
Because it's unusual to
have something that's

01:13:58.210 --> 01:14:01.500
so self-contained, you've
got to have some way

01:14:01.500 --> 01:14:04.610
to let the pressure come and go.

01:14:04.610 --> 01:14:08.120
And I do remember from
the documentary that

01:14:08.120 --> 01:14:11.650
the inhabitants started
getting really sick

01:14:11.650 --> 01:14:13.800
because the oxygen levels were so low

01:14:13.800 --> 01:14:15.033
and that's the first thing

01:14:15.033 --> 01:14:19.000
where they had to get some
outside help for the oxygen.

01:14:19.000 --> 01:14:23.510
The oxygen level ended up
going from like 21 to like 14,

01:14:23.510 --> 01:14:26.510
which makes you a little bit
crazy, and it's also dangerous.

01:14:26.510 --> 01:14:29.240
Yeah, so it didn't quite
work out, but highly,

01:14:29.240 --> 01:14:30.880
and this is right outside of Tucson,

01:14:30.880 --> 01:14:34.620
highly recommended stop, if
you find yourself in that area.

01:14:34.620 --> 01:14:35.650
And to have a cool T-shirt.

01:14:35.650 --> 01:14:36.623
I would like to do that.

01:14:37.710 --> 01:14:41.055
Now, the trip is about to come to a close.

01:14:41.055 --> 01:14:42.376
Yeah, you're getting close home.

01:14:42.376 --> 01:14:46.974
This is the 30th and
the thought was, okay...

01:14:46.974 --> 01:14:47.807
Did you have the time

01:14:47.807 --> 01:14:49.773
to do the retrospective montage?

01:14:50.860 --> 01:14:53.790
No, you've seen quite a bit by this point,

01:14:53.790 --> 01:14:56.600
but we got to go through
Phoenix and the idea was,

01:14:56.600 --> 01:14:58.350
okay, let's just stop in Phoenix

01:14:58.350 --> 01:15:01.260
and then hang out in Phoenix
and see what we can get into

01:15:01.260 --> 01:15:02.540
and then we'll stay in Phoenix.

01:15:02.540 --> 01:15:03.810
But that was also when we looked

01:15:03.810 --> 01:15:07.310
and saw that Phoenix is only
five and a half hours from LA.

01:15:07.310 --> 01:15:10.080
The classic, should we
just get home conversation?

01:15:10.080 --> 01:15:11.840
Well, it wasn't like we weren't
going to stop at Phoenix,

01:15:11.840 --> 01:15:13.190
it was like, do we need
to stay in Phoenix?

01:15:13.190 --> 01:15:15.710
Let's just get home late and
just sleep in our own beds.

01:15:15.710 --> 01:15:18.283
So we kind of determined to do that,

01:15:19.670 --> 01:15:21.140
but there's a couple of
things about Phoenix.

01:15:21.140 --> 01:15:23.983
I don't think I've ever been to Phoenix.

01:15:23.983 --> 01:15:26.240
I've been around it,
they've got a big Belt line.

01:15:26.240 --> 01:15:28.911
Maybe we stopped there
for a tour or something.

01:15:28.911 --> 01:15:30.760
I don't remember everywhere we've been,

01:15:30.760 --> 01:15:33.530
but like fifth largest city in America.

01:15:33.530 --> 01:15:34.363
Did you know that?

01:15:34.363 --> 01:15:35.230
Could not have told you that.

01:15:35.230 --> 01:15:38.580
Behind like LA, New York,
Chicago, and I guess Boston.

01:15:38.580 --> 01:15:40.260
You're talking about square footage?

01:15:40.260 --> 01:15:41.093
People.

01:15:41.093 --> 01:15:41.926
People?

01:15:41.926 --> 01:15:45.410
Yes, and it's also because
it includes like Scottsdale

01:15:45.410 --> 01:15:48.350
and some other places that you
may have heard of as a city,

01:15:48.350 --> 01:15:49.570
but it's also really just...

01:15:49.570 --> 01:15:50.700
Fifth largest?

01:15:50.700 --> 01:15:51.693
It's a giant.

01:15:53.582 --> 01:15:55.080
The thing I noticed about Phoenix is,

01:15:55.080 --> 01:15:58.270
nothing against any of the
other places that I've visited,

01:15:58.270 --> 01:16:01.050
but Phoenix is the first
place where I was like,

01:16:01.050 --> 01:16:06.050
began to recognize like...

01:16:06.450 --> 01:16:08.870
You're searching for the
diplomatic word to say this.

01:16:08.870 --> 01:16:09.703
What is it?

01:16:09.703 --> 01:16:10.540
I'll just say hipsters.

01:16:10.540 --> 01:16:12.006
Okay.

01:16:12.006 --> 01:16:13.631
I wasn't going to say cool people.

01:16:16.320 --> 01:16:18.940
But I started to feel that
there was a proximity...

01:16:18.940 --> 01:16:23.860
A hipster contingent here not
experienced after Nashville.

01:16:23.860 --> 01:16:25.400
Nashville has a hipster component

01:16:25.400 --> 01:16:28.360
but when you go to the
downtown area of Nashville,

01:16:28.360 --> 01:16:30.551
it's like going to Hollywood, California.

01:16:30.551 --> 01:16:32.423
It's like Hollywood, Boulevard.

01:16:34.327 --> 01:16:37.070
No one's from there, except maybe...

01:16:37.070 --> 01:16:39.260
So, you found that in Phoenix?

01:16:39.260 --> 01:16:40.093
Yeah.

01:16:40.093 --> 01:16:42.500
Some too good for their
own good coffee shops?

01:16:42.500 --> 01:16:44.420
Yeah, we're talking in
coffee shops, breweries,

01:16:44.420 --> 01:16:45.253
we ate at this place called,

01:16:45.253 --> 01:16:47.610
I think it was The Wilderness Brewery

01:16:47.610 --> 01:16:49.700
and it was like a bunch of hipsters there

01:16:49.700 --> 01:16:51.790
and it felt very at home.

01:16:51.790 --> 01:16:54.430
So you might be eyeing Phoenix, huh?

01:16:54.430 --> 01:16:57.660
Well, the thing is, it was Roosevelt road,

01:16:57.660 --> 01:16:59.230
which is kind of like their arts district,

01:16:59.230 --> 01:17:01.067
and it was really cool,

01:17:01.067 --> 01:17:05.663
and then I was like, oh,
Phoenix is like a cool place

01:17:05.663 --> 01:17:07.440
that we could come on for like a weekend.

01:17:07.440 --> 01:17:10.670
because it's the same just
driving to San Francisco,

01:17:10.670 --> 01:17:12.210
it's not that bad.

01:17:12.210 --> 01:17:16.260
But then I started looking at
the average daily temperature

01:17:16.260 --> 01:17:17.110
throughout the year

01:17:17.110 --> 01:17:20.140
and there's like six or seven months

01:17:20.140 --> 01:17:21.653
where it's like a hundred.

01:17:22.800 --> 01:17:24.870
It's a hot place, you know?

01:17:24.870 --> 01:17:27.030
So I don't know how they
deal with that exactly

01:17:27.030 --> 01:17:28.242
besides just going inside.

01:17:28.242 --> 01:17:32.260
But it seems like there's some
good times to visit Phoenix.

01:17:35.270 --> 01:17:38.660
But one of the things that I
skipped over in terms of music

01:17:38.660 --> 01:17:42.700
is somewhere at this part of the trip

01:17:42.700 --> 01:17:44.723
is when I'm beginning to think, like,

01:17:46.257 --> 01:17:49.690
I've got to introduce
Locke to some other stuff.

01:17:49.690 --> 01:17:50.642
And that was when I was like,

01:17:50.642 --> 01:17:51.475
"Oh, you know what?

01:17:51.475 --> 01:17:53.530
I think you'll like Diamond Rio."

01:17:53.530 --> 01:17:55.103
After George Strait, yeah.

01:17:56.258 --> 01:17:58.140
Because I was like,
speaking in '90s country,

01:17:58.140 --> 01:17:59.003
I was like, there was these guys

01:17:59.003 --> 01:18:01.590
that Link and I were really, really into,

01:18:01.590 --> 01:18:06.180
and they kind of, I'm
not going to call them

01:18:06.180 --> 01:18:07.040
a flash in the pan.

01:18:07.040 --> 01:18:08.610
They're still together and
they're still making music,

01:18:08.610 --> 01:18:11.060
but the '90s, they peaked
pretty hard in the '90s.

01:18:11.060 --> 01:18:13.620
They had three really solid albums.

01:18:13.620 --> 01:18:18.620
I don't know, I would say four,
had some really good hits.

01:18:20.017 --> 01:18:23.220
And they had a, I don't know
if they were number ones,

01:18:23.220 --> 01:18:24.140
they probably were because

01:18:24.140 --> 01:18:27.060
they had some incredible songs, right?

01:18:27.060 --> 01:18:31.536
Bluegrass infused '90s
country, lots of harmony.

01:18:31.536 --> 01:18:34.520
The vocal approach is the thing

01:18:34.520 --> 01:18:37.910
that is so notable about them.

01:18:37.910 --> 01:18:38.830
And I would say that

01:18:38.830 --> 01:18:40.980
yes, to the bluegrass niche of it all,

01:18:40.980 --> 01:18:42.880
but it doesn't have that,

01:18:42.880 --> 01:18:44.560
it doesn't get into bluegrass

01:18:44.560 --> 01:18:47.850
sort of chord structure and tempo.

01:18:47.850 --> 01:18:51.600
It's like kind of country,
rocky with this...

01:18:51.600 --> 01:18:52.433
Infused.

01:18:52.433 --> 01:18:56.390
This singing, and I could
just look on Locke's face

01:18:56.390 --> 01:18:58.310
as we were listening to
Love a Little Stronger

01:18:58.310 --> 01:18:59.143
and then One More Day,

01:18:59.143 --> 01:19:02.195
wait, didn't have One More
Day play at your wedding?

01:19:02.195 --> 01:19:03.570
♪ One more day♪

01:19:03.570 --> 01:19:07.330
No, that's talking about
if Somebody died, Rhett.

01:19:07.330 --> 01:19:10.780
It's, I Know How the River Feels.

01:19:10.780 --> 01:19:12.320
Oh yeah, yeah, yeah, yeah.

01:19:12.320 --> 01:19:13.153
But one more day, man...

01:19:13.153 --> 01:19:17.290
When you meet your soulmate
and they're still living.

01:19:17.290 --> 01:19:20.640
Okay, well, we'll play it
One More Day at your funeral.

01:19:20.640 --> 01:19:24.963
One More Day is a great song. So Locke...

01:19:26.225 --> 01:19:27.230
There're so many great songs.

01:19:27.230 --> 01:19:28.590
So from that point in the trip,

01:19:28.590 --> 01:19:29.830
so this is like the last two days,

01:19:29.830 --> 01:19:31.040
he just kept wanting to go back

01:19:31.040 --> 01:19:35.370
to those four or five really
amazing Diamond Rio songs.

01:19:35.370 --> 01:19:39.187
And then he's like texting
me recently and was like,

01:19:39.187 --> 01:19:41.637
"Dad, we've got to go to
a Diamond Rio concert."

01:19:42.850 --> 01:19:44.134
So he's all in on Diamond Rio.

01:19:44.134 --> 01:19:49.134
Yeah, definitely top five country artists.

01:19:49.300 --> 01:19:52.010
I think I might have to put
them in my top five of artists,

01:19:52.010 --> 01:19:57.010
not just, I mean, definitely
groups and definitely, '90s.

01:19:58.158 --> 01:20:03.120
Okay, so we're on day five and
we have driven quite a bit,

01:20:03.120 --> 01:20:05.180
but we're also kind of saying
let's sleep in our own beds

01:20:05.180 --> 01:20:07.230
and we just go ahead and we make the trip.

01:20:07.230 --> 01:20:08.063
Power through.

01:20:08.063 --> 01:20:10.030
We get back to LA at like 10:30 at night

01:20:10.030 --> 01:20:14.160
and let me just say that
Locke and I are very similar

01:20:14.160 --> 01:20:19.160
in a lot of ways, which is
a recipe for fights, right?

01:20:21.820 --> 01:20:26.820
And we did not get into,
and of course he's 17.

01:20:26.955 --> 01:20:30.320
Like when we're at home in
our normal everyday lives

01:20:30.320 --> 01:20:32.710
where there's things like,

01:20:32.710 --> 01:20:33.660
you didn't take the trash out.

01:20:33.660 --> 01:20:35.190
There's these lots of little things

01:20:35.190 --> 01:20:37.500
that could then turn into an argument,

01:20:37.500 --> 01:20:41.510
but we got through this trip
without any sort of friction.

01:20:41.510 --> 01:20:43.017
We were having an
incredible time together.

01:20:43.017 --> 01:20:45.510
Listening to music, seeing this stuff,

01:20:45.510 --> 01:20:47.520
we talked about pretty much everything.

01:20:47.520 --> 01:20:52.520
Philosophy, everything and
where we were listening to

01:20:54.080 --> 01:20:58.183
one of my favorite books, Siddhartha,

01:20:59.210 --> 01:21:01.940
which I listened to on my solo trip

01:21:01.940 --> 01:21:04.060
and it was something that he
had been wanting to listen to

01:21:04.060 --> 01:21:07.180
which is just short audio
books, really easy to listen to.

01:21:07.180 --> 01:21:09.790
So, we were having this incredible time

01:21:09.790 --> 01:21:11.860
and then like 20 minutes from home

01:21:11.860 --> 01:21:14.550
is when we got into our
first like argument.

01:21:14.550 --> 01:21:16.170
And it was like, it
escalated really quickly,

01:21:16.170 --> 01:21:17.117
I don't even remember what it was about,

01:21:17.117 --> 01:21:17.950
and then I was like,

01:21:17.950 --> 01:21:22.400
listen, there's no reason
for us to fight right now.

01:21:22.400 --> 01:21:24.120
Let's just get home, let's just get home

01:21:24.120 --> 01:21:26.143
and just forget about this and we did.

01:21:27.950 --> 01:21:30.530
First of all, multiple
times throughout the trip,

01:21:30.530 --> 01:21:31.587
he was like, "I'm so glad I did this.

01:21:31.587 --> 01:21:34.910
I was wrong, I'm glad
you talked me into this."

01:21:34.910 --> 01:21:36.172
He's telling everybody about it

01:21:36.172 --> 01:21:38.680
and how good of a time he had.

01:21:38.680 --> 01:21:41.300
It is the kind of thing that

01:21:41.300 --> 01:21:42.540
he's going to remember forever.

01:21:42.540 --> 01:21:45.123
And I think is more
likely to now lead to us

01:21:45.123 --> 01:21:46.187
doing another trip together,

01:21:46.187 --> 01:21:47.420
maybe for a spring break,

01:21:47.420 --> 01:21:50.090
we don't know exactly
what we're going to do.

01:21:50.090 --> 01:21:54.693
But it was a success,
a father-son success.

01:21:56.710 --> 01:21:57.937
Is there a point where you went like,

01:21:57.937 --> 01:22:00.203
"Okay, well, what'd you learn, son?

01:22:00.203 --> 01:22:02.160
What are you going to remember forever?"

01:22:02.160 --> 01:22:02.993
Well, one of the things

01:22:02.993 --> 01:22:05.530
that I've come to grips with is

01:22:05.530 --> 01:22:08.980
every dad, most dads, I think,

01:22:08.980 --> 01:22:11.290
have a tendency to dadify things,

01:22:11.290 --> 01:22:15.840
and to try to find the lesson
or the moral in something

01:22:15.840 --> 01:22:17.460
and like my kids have become...

01:22:17.460 --> 01:22:19.040
To codify something to feel

01:22:19.040 --> 01:22:21.303
there is an impulse to like,

01:22:21.303 --> 01:22:23.520
let's land this plane

01:22:23.520 --> 01:22:27.483
and I am doing a good job as a dad.

01:22:27.483 --> 01:22:30.173
It's a question that you seek the answer,

01:22:30.173 --> 01:22:31.750
I think that drives that.

01:22:31.750 --> 01:22:33.230
Well, the thing that has
become very clear to me

01:22:33.230 --> 01:22:35.700
is that that is for me
only, that's not for Locke.

01:22:35.700 --> 01:22:36.590
Yeah, right.

01:22:36.590 --> 01:22:41.590
That exercise is 100% for my own ego.

01:22:41.820 --> 01:22:45.170
And own sense of security or...

01:22:45.170 --> 01:22:46.347
Yeah, it could be.

01:22:46.347 --> 01:22:48.570
And I'm using ego broadly.

01:22:48.570 --> 01:22:51.037
So, and I actually made the decision,

01:22:51.037 --> 01:22:55.270
there's no lecturing,
there's no trying to get him

01:22:55.270 --> 01:22:57.370
to see something a
certain way on this trip.

01:22:57.370 --> 01:23:02.170
Now, what I found is that he
actually is very interested

01:23:02.170 --> 01:23:03.150
in what I think about things,

01:23:03.150 --> 01:23:05.340
he's very interested in my opinion.

01:23:05.340 --> 01:23:08.430
And when I don't use every conversation

01:23:08.430 --> 01:23:10.790
as a jumping off point for some lesson

01:23:10.790 --> 01:23:12.150
or for giving him some perspective,

01:23:12.150 --> 01:23:15.660
he just naturally asks
me or runs things by me

01:23:15.660 --> 01:23:17.540
in a way that it's very
clear that what he's after

01:23:17.540 --> 01:23:18.695
is what I think about this.

01:23:18.695 --> 01:23:20.270
Now, I have to be really careful

01:23:20.270 --> 01:23:21.770
once I start talking about those things

01:23:21.770 --> 01:23:24.360
to not turn it into a lecture

01:23:24.360 --> 01:23:26.140
and to make it more a, well, I mean,

01:23:26.140 --> 01:23:27.141
one way to see that is this,

01:23:27.141 --> 01:23:29.391
or here's one thing that
I would think about.

01:23:31.910 --> 01:23:35.000
We really had some really
great conversations

01:23:35.000 --> 01:23:37.650
about a lot of things
that we kind of talk about

01:23:37.650 --> 01:23:38.910
from time to time

01:23:38.910 --> 01:23:42.530
but in the setting of this,
where there's no place to go,

01:23:42.530 --> 01:23:43.733
we've got to kill time,

01:23:46.660 --> 01:23:48.707
it lent itself to those
kinds of conversations

01:23:48.707 --> 01:23:51.500
and he was very much all for it.

01:23:51.500 --> 01:23:54.230
I mean, it was great for our relationship.

01:23:54.230 --> 01:23:57.630
And now that we're back in the
swing of just regular life,

01:23:57.630 --> 01:24:00.133
yes, there's been some arguments again,

01:24:01.480 --> 01:24:04.017
but I think that there's a connection

01:24:04.017 --> 01:24:06.530
that already there, of course,

01:24:06.530 --> 01:24:09.770
but it was just strengthened
and enhanced through this.

01:24:09.770 --> 01:24:11.960
So, I highly recommended
if you have the opportunity

01:24:11.960 --> 01:24:13.493
to take a trip.

01:24:13.493 --> 01:24:14.326
With my son.

01:24:14.326 --> 01:24:15.890
That's my trip.

01:24:15.890 --> 01:24:20.890
My rec is one other, is the
album that we listened to

01:24:22.160 --> 01:24:26.723
more than any album that we
both just fell in love with.

01:24:27.810 --> 01:24:29.480
I think I have told you about this guy,

01:24:29.480 --> 01:24:30.313
but I don't know how much

01:24:30.313 --> 01:24:31.930
you've listened to him, Brent Cobb.

01:24:31.930 --> 01:24:32.763
Yeah.

01:24:32.763 --> 01:24:34.360
He's got a song that's really popular,

01:24:34.360 --> 01:24:37.170
like Keep Them on their
Toes or something like that.

01:24:37.170 --> 01:24:38.003
Yeah.

01:24:38.003 --> 01:24:39.100
And like any artist,

01:24:39.100 --> 01:24:40.869
there are some albums or some songs

01:24:40.869 --> 01:24:42.970
that don't really resonate,

01:24:42.970 --> 01:24:45.700
but he's got an album
from a few years back

01:24:45.700 --> 01:24:48.490
called Shine on a Rainy Day.

01:24:48.490 --> 01:24:52.130
And this is the one that
we just centered in on

01:24:52.130 --> 01:24:54.590
and I like his most recent album as well,

01:24:54.590 --> 01:24:56.420
but he's a south Georgia boy,

01:24:56.420 --> 01:24:59.000
who's born very close to where I was born,

01:24:59.000 --> 01:25:02.410
he's got a song about, I
think called South of Atlanta,

01:25:02.410 --> 01:25:04.170
which is about going back to his hometown

01:25:04.170 --> 01:25:05.200
and I think he now lives,

01:25:05.200 --> 01:25:06.806
he was in Nashville for a while,

01:25:06.806 --> 01:25:07.639
but now he lives down
there in South Georgia.

01:25:07.639 --> 01:25:08.920
It's a laid back, this album?

01:25:08.920 --> 01:25:09.753
Yeah, that's the thing,

01:25:09.753 --> 01:25:14.649
is that he'll go a little
bit rock in some places,

01:25:14.649 --> 01:25:17.060
which is why I don't like as much,

01:25:17.060 --> 01:25:19.230
on this album is almost exclusively,

01:25:19.230 --> 01:25:24.230
just like thoughtful sort
of soulful, good country.

01:25:25.810 --> 01:25:27.900
And again, this isn't the kind of country

01:25:27.900 --> 01:25:30.070
you're going to hear on country radio.

01:25:30.070 --> 01:25:31.630
This is way better than that.

01:25:32.613 --> 01:25:33.880
And it's just so amazing to me

01:25:33.880 --> 01:25:35.480
that they don't play
this music on the radio,

01:25:35.480 --> 01:25:40.230
but Brent Cobb, Georgia boy, huge fan.

01:25:40.230 --> 01:25:43.980
Shine on a Rainy Day just
starts off with a song

01:25:43.980 --> 01:25:47.290
about sitting on a porch

01:25:47.290 --> 01:25:48.740
and solving the problems of the world

01:25:48.740 --> 01:25:51.023
and he just goes on from there.

01:25:52.150 --> 01:25:53.215
So that's my rec.

01:25:53.215 --> 01:25:55.630
Great road trip music,

01:25:55.630 --> 01:25:57.395
but also just great music in general.

01:25:57.395 --> 01:26:00.790
I'm glad he had him, glad he made it.

01:26:00.790 --> 01:26:02.920
Yeah, relationship didn't fall off.

01:26:02.920 --> 01:26:04.140
Not only intact,

01:26:04.140 --> 01:26:07.250
but you've got some memories
that will last his lifetime.

01:26:07.250 --> 01:26:09.940
Of course, you're going
to pass on at some point

01:26:09.940 --> 01:26:11.320
and then you'll live on

01:26:11.320 --> 01:26:13.900
a little bit of how to
live on in his memory.

01:26:13.900 --> 01:26:16.000
That's true and in video form,

01:26:16.000 --> 01:26:18.150
for those of you who watched the video.

01:26:18.150 --> 01:26:20.550
There's a lot to see,

01:26:20.550 --> 01:26:21.570
that Link didn't even get to see.

